<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Boomplaats</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Boomplaats was fought on 29 August 1848 in the south of the present-day Free State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, between British troops under Sir Harry Smith and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a>. The British victory confirmed the annexation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> withdrew from the territory in 1854, allowing the Orange Free State to be founded.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-boomplaats.jpg" alt="Battle of Boomplaats" width="320" height="532" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bataille_de_Boomplaats.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Sir Harry Smith became Governor of the Cape in 1847 and began to undo the agreements his predecessor, Sir Peregrine Maitland, had made with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griqua</a> leaders over the land between the Vaal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange Rivers</a>. Smith had assured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a> that he would not proclaim the territory British until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> had been consulted, but before that consultation was complete he annexed the land between the Vaal, the Orange and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> on 3 February 1848 as the Orange River Sovereignty.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> at Winburg appealed to Pretorius, who by June 1848 had raised a commando of around 1,000 men. He drove the British magistrate Thomas Biddulph out of Winburg, declared a republic and advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>, where the British Resident, Major Henry Warden, held the town with 70 men of the Cape Corps. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> stayed neutral, and Warden withdrew to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a>. A British force formed under Colonel George Buller marched by way of Colesberg and crossed the Orange unopposed by 26 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On the morning of 29 August the British column halted at the farm of Touwfontein, where Smith learned that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> held low hills about 19 km away, near a farm called Boomplaats. Smith's force included the Cape Mounted Rifles, companies of the Rifle Brigade, the 45th Regiment and the 91st Highlanders, three Royal Artillery field guns and 200 to 250 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griqua">Griquas</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-waterboer">Andries Waterboer</a> and Adam Kok; the Boers numbered 300 to 500. A reconnaissance party led by Smith was surprised on a ridge when a Boer opened fire too early and gave away their position.</p><p>The 45th Regiment in the centre came under heavy fire and took its objectives only with the help of the reserve, while the Rifle Brigade took the right. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> attempt to turn the British left and reach the wagon train was stopped by the Cape Mounted Rifles. After about four hours of fighting from ridge to ridge, Pretorius retreated toward Winburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Losses are disputed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> memorials record 9 killed and 7 wounded, while a British notice of the time gave 49 rebels killed; British memorials record 16 killed. The captured Boer Thomas Frederik Hermanus Dreyer was court-martialled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> and shot on 4 September 1848, alongside the British deserter Michael Quigley. Pretorius withdrew across the Vaal with a bounty of £1,000 on his head. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sand-river-convention">Sand River Convention</a> of 17 January 1852 recognised a Boer republic north of the Vaal, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein-convention">Bloemfontein Convention</a> of 23 February 1854 gave up the territory between the Vaal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange Rivers</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Boomplaats” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In February 1848 the Cape governor Sir Harry Smith proclaimed British sovereignty over the region as the Orange River Sovereignty, and he defeated Boer resistance under Andries Pretorius at the <b>Battle of Boomplaats</b> on 29 August 1848.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Andries Pretorius rose against the annexation but was defeated at <b>Boomplaats</b> in August 1848.</blockquote><a href="/state/orange-river-sovereignty">Orange River Sovereignty</a></li><li><blockquote>Britain declared the Orange River Sovereignty in 1848 and defeated Andries Pretorius at <b>Boomplaats</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-winburg">Republic of Winburg</a></li></ul></section>
